    expectedAnswer: "LIS asks for the longest subsequence whose values are strictly increasing. A straightforward DP defines dp[i] as the longest increasing subsequence ending at index i. An optimized approach uses a tails array and binary search.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE O(n²) DP:\n```ts\nfunction lengthOfLIS(nums: readonly number[]): number {\n  if (nums.length === 0) return 0;\n\n  const dp = new Array<number>(nums.length).fill(1);\n  let best = 1;\n\n  for (let i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {\n      if (nums[j]! < nums[i]!) {\n        dp[i] = Math.max(dp[i]!, dp[j]! + 1);\n      }\n    }\n\n    best = Math.max(best, dp[i]!);\n  }\n\n  return best;\n}\n```\n\nStep 2 — WITH BUILT-IN / binary-search helper:\n```ts\nfunction lowerBound(nums: readonly number[], target: number): number {\n  let left = 0;\n  let right = nums.length;\n\n  while (left < right) {\n    const middle = left + Math.floor((right - left) / 2);\n    if (nums[middle]! < target) left = middle + 1;\n    else right = middle;\n  }\n\n  return left;\n}\n\nfunction lengthOfLISOptimized(nums: readonly number[]): number {\n  const tails: number[] = [];\n\n  for (const value of nums) {\n    const index = lowerBound(tails, value);\n    tails[index] = value;\n  }\n\n  return tails.length;\n}\n```\n\nStep 3 — Example:\n[10,9,2,5,3,7,101,18]\nLIS length = 4, e.g. [2,3,7,101].\n\nStep 4 — Complexity:\nO(n²) DP.\nOptimized tails + binary search O(n log n).\nThe `tails` array is not itself the final LIS sequence without reconstruction metadata.",
