    expectedAnswer: "State-transition design is the core DP skill: choose the minimum state that contains enough information to determine future decisions, define transitions from previous states, establish base cases, and choose a valid evaluation order.",
    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — A reusable state design checklist:\n1. What does one state mean?\n2. Which previous states can reach it?\n3. What choice/transition connects them?\n4. What are the base states?\n5. Is the state acyclic and finitely bounded?\n6. Can the answer be reconstructed from predecessor choices?\n\nStep 2 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE example: maximum profit with one stock transaction.\n```ts\nfunction maxProfit(prices: readonly number[]): number {\n  if (prices.length === 0) return 0;\n\n  let minPrice = prices[0]!;\n  let bestProfit = 0;\n\n  for (let i = 1; i < prices.length; i++) {\n    bestProfit = Math.max(\n      bestProfit,\n      prices[i]! - minPrice,\n    );\n\n    minPrice = Math.min(minPrice, prices[i]!);\n  }\n\n  return bestProfit;\n}\n```\n\nStep 3 — WITH BUILT-IN:\n```ts\nfunction maxProfitUsingReduce(prices: readonly number[]): number {\n  return prices.reduce(\n    (state, price) => ({\n      minPrice: Math.min(state.minPrice, price),\n      bestProfit: Math.max(state.bestProfit, price - state.minPrice),\n    }),\n    {\n      minPrice: Infinity,\n      bestProfit: 0,\n    },\n  ).bestProfit;\n}\n```\n\nStep 4 — Example:\n[7,1,5,3,6,4]\nbuy at 1, sell at 6 → profit 5.\n\nDP lesson:\nEven when a problem can be compressed into a few variables, the correct way to discover the compression is to first understand the state and transition.",
